<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<!-- 2. INTRODUCTION -->
<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<h1>2. Introduction</h1>
<p>
Across higher-education residential campuses housing tens of thousands of students and faculty members, personal belongings such as identification cards, smartphones, laptops, backpacks, keys, and scientific calculators are frequently misplaced. In a university with multiple sprawling academic blocks, multi-level central libraries, laboratories, sporting complexes, and food courts, recovering lost property is a daunting logistical task.
</p>
<p>
Historically, campus lost-and-found management has relied on physical logbooks maintained by individual security guards at various entrance gates or fragmented informal posts across social media groups. These manual systems suffer from critical flaws:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Lack of Centralized Discovery:</b> A student who misplaces an item in an academic hall has no centralized way of discovering that the item was turned into a security desk on the other side of campus.</li>
  <li><b>Susceptibility to Unauthorized Claims:</b> Valuable electronics and branded items are frequently claimed by unauthorized individuals due to the absence of challenge-response proof questions.</li>
  <li><b>Storage Overflow:</b> Security lockers remain cluttered with unclaimed items indefinitely due to the lack of an automated archiving lifecycle.</li>
  <li><b>Vulnerability to Physical Damage:</b> Paper registers can be torn, smudged, or lost, leaving no traceable audit record for university authorities.</li>
</ul>
<p>
The <b>Campus Lost &amp; Found Management System</b> is an enterprise-modeled terminal software application developed entirely in standard Java SE. The system combines intuitive business workflows with robust MySQL relational persistence and asynchronous multithreaded daemon scanning.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<!-- 3. PROBLEM STATEMENT -->
<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<h1>3. Problem Statement</h1>
<div class="highlight-box">
<b>Core Problem Statement:</b><br>
"To design and engineer an automated, secure, and reliable Java management system that coordinates the reporting, storage, discovery, verification, and claiming of lost and found campus belongings while maintaining durable database records and background archival surveillance."
</div>
<p>
The project directly addresses the operational bottlenecks of university campuses:
</p>
<ol>
  <li><b>Real-Time Digital Registration:</b> Replaces illegible manual logbooks with structured data entry for both lost reports and found items.</li>
  <li><b>Challenge-Question Verification Engine:</b> Requires claimants to answer specific ownership questions (e.g., sticker details, wallpaper description, contents of wallet) before campus staff release the property.</li>
  <li><b>Asynchronous Background Archiving:</b> Employs an independent daemon thread that scans inventory every few seconds to identify and archive items unclaimed for over thirty days without interrupting interactive console sessions.</li>
  <li><b>Auditability &amp; Reporting:</b> Generates permanent recovery summaries and audit logs using Java Character Streams for administrative review.</li>
</ol>

<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<!-- 4. FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS -->
<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<h1>4. Functional Requirements</h1>
<p>The system is organized into three major functional modules in strict compliance with the project guidelines:</p>

<h3>4.1 Module 1: Item Reporting &amp; Inventory Management (CRUD)</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>FR 1.1:</b> Enable students to register <b>Lost Items</b> with attributes including Item Title, Category, Description, Estimated Lost Location, Contact Email, Last-Seen Spot, Student Roll Number, and Optional Monetary Reward.</li>
  <li><b>FR 1.2:</b> Enable security personnel or good samaritans to register <b>Found Items</b> with attributes including Storage Locker Location, Handover Officer Name, and an Ownership Verification Challenge Question.</li>
  <li><b>FR 1.3:</b> Display all active inventory listings categorized by status (<code>UNCLAIMED</code>, <code>PENDING_VERIFICATION</code>, <code>CLAIMED</code>, <code>EXPIRED_ARCHIVED</code>).</li>
  <li><b>FR 1.4:</b> Allow administrators to remove or update obsolete records via standard CRUD operations.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.2 Module 2: Search, Claim &amp; Ownership Verification Engine</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>FR 2.1:</b> Provide real-time keyword search against titles, descriptions, categories, and campus locations.</li>
  <li><b>FR 2.2:</b> Maintain a search history using a <code>java.util.Stack</code>, enabling users to inspect recent searches or pop keywords via an <code>UNDO</code> command.</li>
  <li><b>FR 2.3:</b> Allow students to submit ownership claims by entering their name, roll number, phone contact, and response to the verification challenge question.</li>
  <li><b>FR 2.4:</b> Provide staff with a resolution portal to review submitted proof answers and either approve handovers (status transitions to <code>CLAIMED</code>) or reject claims (status reverts to <code>UNCLAIMED</code>).</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.3 Module 3: Reporting &amp; File I/O Export</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>FR 3.1:</b> Export comprehensive monthly recovery audit summaries to external text files using Java Character Streams (<code>PrintWriter</code>, <code>BufferedWriter</code>, <code>FileWriter</code>).</li>
  <li><b>FR 3.2:</b> Provide an in-console preview feature utilizing <code>BufferedReader</code> to read and render generated reports.</li>
  <li><b>FR 3.3:</b> Report metrics on total items archived by the background multithreaded daemon.</li>
</ul>

<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<!-- 5. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS -->
<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<h1>5. Non-Functional Requirements</h1>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width: 25%;">Requirement</th><th>Specification &amp; Technical Implementation</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td><b>1. Performance</b></td>
    <td>Low-latency execution using in-memory <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> and <code>ArrayList</code> caching, combined with background multithreaded daemon processing (<code>AutoArchiveWorkerThread</code>) that operates asynchronously without locking the user interface.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><b>2. Error Handling</b></td>
    <td>Targeted custom checked exception hierarchy (<code>LostFoundException</code>, <code>ItemNotFoundException</code>, <code>UnauthorizedClaimException</code>) and comprehensive <code>try-catch</code> input validation preventing runtime application crashes.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><b>3. Reliability &amp; Persistence</b></td>
    <td>Durable storage using relational MySQL database tables via standard JDBC drivers with SQL <code>PreparedStatement</code> parameter binding. Includes graceful in-memory fallback if MySQL is offline.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><b>4. Maintainability</b></td>
    <td>Clean, layered package architecture (<code>model</code>, <code>dao</code>, <code>service</code>, <code>exception</code>, <code>thread</code>, <code>util</code>) promoting loose coupling, high cohesion, and readable code.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<!-- 6. SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE -->
<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<h1>6. System Architecture</h1>
<p>The system follows a classic 3-Tier Layered Architecture:</p>
<div class="diagram-box">
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                       PRESENTATION & TERMINAL UI LAYER                        |
|  • Main.java (Interactive Console Menus, Scanner I/O, Simulation Runner)       |
+---------------------------------------+---------------------------------------+
                                        |
+---------------------------------------v---------------------------------------+
|                         SERVICE / BUSINESS LOGIC LAYER                        |
|  • LostFoundService / LostFoundServiceImpl (Workflow Coordination)            |
|  • SearchHistoryStack (Collections Stack for Recent Keyword Queries & Undo)   |
+-------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-------------------+
                    |                   |                   |
+-------------------v---+   +-----------v-----------+   +---v-------------------+
|  CONCURRENCY LAYER    |   |  DATA ACCESS LAYER    |   |   STREAM I/O LAYER    |
| • AutoArchiveWorker   |   | • ItemDAO             |   | • ReportExporter      |
| • Daemon Thread       |   | • ItemDAOImpl         |   | • PrintWriter/FileWriter|
| • Synchronized Scans  |   | • DatabaseManager     |   | • BufferedReader      |
| • Lifecycle (Sleep)   |   | • MySQL JDBC Drivers  |   | • Byte/Char Streams   |
+-----------------------+   +-----------------------+   +-----------------------+
</div>

