<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<!-- 8. DESIGN DECISIONS & RATIONALE -->
<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<h1>8. Design Decisions &amp; Rationale</h1>
<ol>
  <li><b>Encapsulated Base Class with Method Overriding:</b> The abstract class <code>Item</code> centralizes common attributes (identifier, category, report date, location) while delegating specialized behavior to <code>LostItem</code> and <code>FoundItem</code> via <code>getDetailsSummary()</code>. This eliminates code duplication and ensures clean polymorphism.</li>
  <li><b>Thread-Safe Singleton Connection Provider:</b> Creating a single shared database manager (<code>DatabaseManager.java</code>) prevents resource leaks, optimizes socket connections, and guarantees unified configuration loading from <code>config/db.properties</code>.</li>
  <li><b>Dual-Mode Storage Architecture (Zero-Failure Guarantee):</b> To ensure that evaluators can run the project on any computer regardless of whether a local MySQL server is installed, the data access layer seamlessly operates in-memory if external database drivers are absent.</li>
  <li><b>Collections Stack for Search History:</b> Employing <code>java.util.Stack</code> provides an intuitive, real-world application of LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) operations by enabling users to review previous keywords and execute search backtracking via <code>undoLastSearch()</code>.</li>
  <li><b>Character Stream File I/O:</b> Monthly audit reports are generated using <code>PrintWriter</code> and <code>BufferedWriter</code> to ensure formatted, cross-platform text files that can be inspected directly or printed.</li>
</ol>

