<!-- 11. TESTING APPROACH -->
<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<h1>11. Testing Approach &amp; Verification</h1>
<p>
Testing was conducted through a standalone automated test harness (<code>LostFoundValidationTest.java</code>) executing 15 verification tests across all syllabus units:
</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Test Category</th><th>Validation Criteria</th><th style="width: 20%;">Status</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Unit 1 Tests</td><td>Ternary operators, boolean expressions, break &amp; continue statements</td><td>PASSED</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Unit 2 Tests</td><td>Inheritance (<code>instanceof</code>), polymorphic overriding, enum constructors</td><td>PASSED</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Unit 3 Tests</td><td>Custom exception throwing &amp; catching, daemon thread lifecycle &amp; sleep</td><td>PASSED</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Unit 4 Tests</td><td><code>ArrayList</code> mutations, <code>Stack</code> push/pop/peek, file writing/reading</td><td>PASSED</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Unit 5 Tests</td><td>JDBC PreparedStatement parameter binding, CRUD operations</td><td>PASSED</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Total Pass Rate: 15 / 15 Tests Passed (100.0% Compliance)</b></p>

<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<!-- 12. CHALLENGES FACED -->
<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<h1>12. Challenges Faced</h1>
<ol>
  <li><b>Cross-Platform Database Portability:</b> Requiring evaluators to run an active MySQL instance on custom ports often causes setup failures during viva demonstrations. This was overcome by implementing a dual-mode persistence architecture with graceful in-memory caching.</li>
  <li><b>Thread Safety During Concurrent Updates:</b> The background auto-archival thread can inspect item records while a student is submitting a claim. Applying synchronized blocks to critical sections in <code>AutoArchiveWorkerThread.java</code> prevented race conditions.</li>
  <li><b>Scanner Input Buffer Cleaning:</b> Reading primitive data types (integers/doubles) followed by text strings in terminal applications can lead to skipped prompts due to residual newline characters. Systematically using <code>scanner.nextLine().trim()</code> eliminated this issue.</li>
</ol>

<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<!-- 13. LEARNINGS & KEY TAKEAWAYS -->
<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<h1>13. Learnings &amp; Key Takeaways</h1>
<ul>
  <li><b>Applied Object-Oriented Modeling:</b> Translating real-world entities into abstract base classes and subclasses solidified our understanding of inheritance and polymorphism.</li>
  <li><b>Concurrent Programming in Java:</b> Implementing a background daemon worker thread provided hands-on experience with thread lifecycles, daemon states, and monitor synchronization.</li>
  <li><b>Data Structure Selection:</b> Selecting <code>ArrayList</code> for query operations and <code>Stack</code> for query history demonstrated the importance of matching data structures to functional requirements.</li>
  <li><b>Relational Persistence:</b> Designing table schemas and utilizing the DAO pattern with JDBC PreparedStatements emphasized the value of decoupled database access.</li>
</ul>

<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<!-- 14. FUTURE ENHANCEMENTS -->
<!-- ============================================================================== -->
<h1>14. Future Enhancements</h1>
<ol>
  <li><b>Computer Vision Matching:</b> Incorporating image processing to automatically match photographs of lost items against turned-in found belongings.</li>
  <li><b>SMS &amp; Email Notifications:</b> Integrating cloud communication APIs (such as Twilio or JavaMail) to send real-time alerts when a matching item is logged.</li>
  <li><b>Barcode Locker Labels:</b> Printing QR codes or barcode labels for physical storage lockers in campus security rooms to accelerate belongings retrieval.</li>
</ol>

